# Family Portal environment
#
# Copy to .env for local development, or to /srv/apps/family/shared/.env on the
# server. Never commit the filled-in file — .env is gitignored, this file is not.
#
# A release build refuses to start when a required setting is missing or
# malformed (backend/config_check.go). A local build logs the same list and
# keeps going, so a development machine needs almost none of this.
#
# No real values below. Every line is a placeholder or an example.

# ── Required in release ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Public origin, scheme and host only: no trailing slash, no path. Used to build
# Google's OAuth redirect, password-reset links, and the allowed WebSocket
# origin, so a wrong value sends users somewhere that is not this site. Must be
# https in release.
SITE_ROOT=https://example.com

# Signing key for session JWTs. At least 32 characters, random. Changing it logs
# everyone out; leaving it unset in release is a startup failure, and leaving it
# unset locally generates a throwaway key at each boot.
#   openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET_KEY=

# Google sign-in. The login page offers the button unconditionally, so a missing
# credential is a dead button rather than a hidden feature. Create an OAuth 2.0
# Web application client in the Google Cloud console and add
# $SITE_ROOT/api/google/callback as an authorized redirect URI.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Gemini key for AI-assisted import, which is exposed to every user in the UI.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Bearer token guarding /internal/snapshot, the endpoint the nightly backup
# reads the database through. At least 32 characters. Unset in release is a
# startup failure: an endpoint that 404s because nobody set a token is a backup
# that silently never runs. Must match snapshot_token_var in
# /srv/apps/family/shared/backup.conf.
#   openssl rand -base64 48
BACKUP_TOKEN=

# Outbound mail. Password reset is the only way a locked-out user gets back in
# without an operator, so this is not an optional nicety. Two arrangements work:
#
#   1. MAIL_FROM alone — hand mail to a local relay that authenticates nothing.
#      This is the server setup: Postfix listens on 127.0.0.1:25 and OpenDKIM
#      signs, so SMTP_HOST and SMTP_PORT arrive from a systemd drop-in and
#      belong in neither this file nor the repo.
#   2. EMAIL and APP_PASSWORD — authenticate to a submission host and send as
#      EMAIL. Defaults to Gmail's submission host when SMTP_HOST is unset.
MAIL_FROM=noreply@example.com
#EMAIL=
#APP_PASSWORD=
#SMTP_HOST=127.0.0.1
#SMTP_PORT=25

# ── Optional ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Overrides the Gemini model used for AI import. Defaults to
# models/gemini-2.5-flash (backend/ai.go, GetDefaultAIModel).
#AI_MODEL=

# iOS OAuth client for the companion app. Not part of 1.0; unset is fine.
#GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=

# Turns off rate limiting. Honored only in local builds — a release build always
# limits. For load testing and end-to-end runs, where the limits would otherwise
# be the thing under test.
#RATE_LIMIT_DISABLED=1

# Listening port, read by the deploy tool's health check and by the Caddy vhost
# it writes. The Go binary itself listens on a compile-time constant 8666
# (release/release.go), so this must be 8666 for the two to agree.
#PORT=8666

# ── Apple push notifications (companion app, 1.1) ──────────────────────────────
#
# All-or-nothing: configuring none of these is expected in 1.0, and configuring
# some of them fails startup in release. A half-configured APNs looks enabled
# and rejects every device registration, in the least visible place possible.
#APNS_TEAM_ID=
#APNS_KEY_ID=
#APNS_BUNDLE_ID=com.example.app
#APNS_KEY_PATH=/srv/apps/family/shared/apns/AuthKey_XXXXXXXXXX.p8
#APNS_ENVIRONMENT=sandbox   # sandbox | production
